result:
Session-based login is the practical choice for a small Express app. Users log in once, the server stores their identity in a session, and a signed cookie carries the session ID. Passwords are stored only as bcrypt hashes, and a middleware function guards the protected routes.

## Steps

1. Install the packages:
   ```bash
   npm install express express-session bcrypt
   ```
2. Replace the in-memory `users` array below with a database table (`id`, `username` unique, `password_hash`) when you're ready.
3. Set a real secret in your environment: `SESSION_SECRET=<long random string>`.
4. Run `node app.js` and visit `/dashboard`. You'll be redirected to `/login`.

## The code

```js
// app.js
const express = require('express');
const session = require('express-session');
const bcrypt = require('bcrypt');

const app = express();
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false })); // read HTML form posts

// Session setup: stores a signed cookie in the browser
app.use(session({
  secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET || 'dev-only-change-me',
  resave: false,
  saveUninitialized: false,           // don't create sessions for anonymous visitors
  cookie: {
    httpOnly: true,                   // JavaScript in the page can't read the cookie
    sameSite: 'lax',                  // blocks most cross-site request forgery
    secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production', // HTTPS only in production
    maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 2        // log out after 2 hours
  }
}));

// Demo user store. Replace with a database lookup.
// bcrypt.hashSync creates the hash once at startup; never store plain-text passwords.
const users = [
  { username: 'alice', passwordHash: bcrypt.hashSync('correct-horse-battery', 12) }
];
// Used to keep timing similar when the username doesn't exist
const DUMMY_HASH = bcrypt.hashSync('not-a-real-password', 12);

// Middleware: only lets logged-in users continue
function requireLogin(req, res, next) {
  if (req.session.user) return next();
  res.redirect('/login');
}

// Escape text before putting it into HTML
const esc = s => String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g, c =>
  ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;', "'": '&#39;' }[c]));

app.get('/', (req, res) => res.redirect('/dashboard'));

app.get('/login', (req, res) => {
  res.send(`
    <h1>Log in</h1>
    <form method="post" action="/login">
      <input name="username" placeholder="Username" required autofocus>
      <input name="password" type="password" placeholder="Password" required>
      <button>Log in</button>
    </form>`);
});

app.post('/login', async (req, res) => {
  const { username = '', password = '' } = req.body;
  const user = users.find(u => u.username === username);

  // Always run bcrypt so an attacker can't tell which usernames exist
  const ok = await bcrypt.compare(password, user ? user.passwordHash : DUMMY_HASH);
  if (!user || !ok) return res.status(401).send('Invalid username or password.');

  // Regenerate the session ID on login to prevent session fixation attacks
  req.session.regenerate(err => {
    if (err) return res.status(500).send('Login failed, please try again.');
    req.session.user = { username: user.username };
    res.redirect('/dashboard');
  });
});

app.post('/logout', (req, res) => {
  req.session.destroy(() => {
    res.clearCookie('connect.sid');
    res.redirect('/login');
  });
});

// Protected routes: add requireLogin before the handler
app.get('/dashboard', requireLogin, (req, res) => {
  res.send(`
    <h1>Welcome, ${esc(req.session.user.username)}</h1>
    <form method="post" action="/logout"><button>Log out</button></form>`);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Running at http://localhost:3000'));
```

## Before going to production

- **Session store:** The default in-memory store loses all sessions on restart and doesn't scale past one process. Use a store such as `connect-pg-simple` (Postgres) or `connect-redis`.
- **Rate limiting:** Add `express-rate-limit` on `POST /login` to slow down password guessing.
- **CSRF tokens:** `sameSite: 'lax'` covers most cases, but for stricter protection use a token library such as `csrf-sync`. `csurf` is deprecated.
- **HTTPS:** Required for `secure: true` cookies to work. Most hosts provide it.
- **Password rules:** Enforce a minimum length (at least 12 characters is a sensible default) and never log passwords.
- **Trust proxy:** If you run behind a reverse proxy or load balancer, set `app.set('trust proxy', 1)` so secure cookies and rate limiting see the correct client address.

## Alternatives

- **Passport.js with `passport-local`:** Adds a standard strategy structure. Useful if you'll later add Google or GitHub login, but it's more setup for a simple app.
- **Managed auth (Clerk, Auth0, Supabase Auth):** Handles password resets, email verification, MFA, and breach checks for you. Often the better choice if you don't want to maintain auth code.

If you tell me where the app will be hosted and what database you use, I can adapt the user store and session store to match.
